Z||000015||RFMike||12-04-1999||05:46 AM||||JH-<BR>If the GT is mechanically sound and appears to be well maintained, go for it.  You won't regret it.  Check out everything you can- exhaust, shocks, wheel play, engine compression, plug wires, vacuum lines, look at the tail pipes for signs of oil burning, brake feel, general condition of interior and exterior.  You get the picture.  If a lot of things need attention, these are your bargaining chips.  If they don't, I think the price is good.  That's about what I paid for a comparable 86 GT that was in top condition.<P>Cooter, you gotta be kidding!  If that 88GT is in good condition, it would bring twice that where I live.  When you're wife sees how much enjoyment you get out of it, her heart will overflow with happiness for you.<BR>Keep telling yourself that.  Har-har-har.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Sage||12-04-1999||08:32 AM||||Kel said it all!  Look it over real good, and try to determine, how much more you will put in to make it "right" or the way yo want it. "We ain't here that long, and you ain't takin nothin withya!" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroshop||12-04-1999||10:09 AM||||Guys, The price for an 86 GT is a fair price. I should know since I buy and restore Fieros and then sell them. I just sold a restored SE for more than what you are going to pay the dealer. One thing to remember is that when you buy from a dealer the dealer has to make sure that the Fiero has passed smog and a safety check, which means IE: Brakes, tires, and anything pertaining to safety. I would say go for it and that you are getting a fair deal.||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||12-04-1999||11:10 AM||||Well, you do have to take into account the area in which you live. The same exact car will probably sell for much more in California where the cost of living is extremely high. Down in Georgia, those good ol' boys ain't used to paying high prices for nuthin'. I'm always finding real cheap Fieros here in Kansas, but they're usually ragged out notchback. I could have quite a collection of parts cars if I wanted.||||reg||1
Z||000020||GT Bastard||12-05-1999||01:30 PM||||Remember, you're not just buying transportation.... You're buying a piece of history that will be forgotten if some gangster-wannabee buys it and stuffs 231 subwoofers in it and then raps it around a tree while drunk.  I paid more for my car that most people think I should have, but they're not easy to find, and it was exactly what I wanted.  The money really didn't <BR>make any difference.  If I hadn't used it for <BR>my Fiero, it would have gone elsewhere. Who <BR>cares if the paint is faded? Get it a paint job and make it look brand new!  I say GO FOR IT!||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jim Baumann||12-05-1999||04:35 PM||||Sounds like a fair price to me I just bought a 86 GT for $1000 but I knew that it needed engine work (thought just a valve job but turned out to be a head gasket), a new clutch and also a paint job. But the interior is in very good shape as well as the rest of the drive train.  I agree with the rest check out the engine real well.||||reg||10
